              "@id": "urn:uuid:9820ec12-91d7-583b-9ba4-d85f7948d14a",
              "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedString",
              "uco-observable:stringValue": "Case 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 1 of 34 PageID.1186\n\nKENNETH M. SORENSON\nUnited States Attorney\nDistrict of Hawaii\n\nREBECCA A. PERLMUTTER\nAssistant United States Attorney\nGWENDELYNN BILLS\nTrial Attorney\nRoom 6-100, PJKK Federal Building\n300 Ala Moana Boulevard\nHonolulu, Hawaii 96850\nTelephone: (808) 541-2850\nEmail: Rebecca.Perlmutter@usdoj.gov\nEmail: Gwendelynn.E.Bills@usdoj.gov\n\nAttorneys for Plaintiff\nUNITED STATES OF AMERICA\n\nIN THE UNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT\n\nFOR THE DISTRICT OF HAWAII\n\nUNITED STATES OF AMERICA, ) CASE NO. CR23-00071 JMS\n)\nPlaintiff, ) GOVERNMENT\u2019S TRIAL BRIEF;\n) CERTIFICATE OF SERVICE\nvs. )\n)\nDARREN PATRICK RILEY, )\n)\nDefendant. )\n)\n\nGOVERNMENT\u2019S TRIAL BRIEF\n\ni\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 2 of 34 PageID.1187\n\nTable of Contents\n\nI. STATEMENT OF THE CASE ...........................................................................1\nA. Superseding Indictment ................................................................................1\nB. Trial Status ...................................................................................................2\nC. Pretrial Rulings .............................................................................................2\nD. Pending Rulings ...........................................................................................2\nII. ANTICIPATED FACTS AND PRESENTATION AT TRIAL .....................3\nA. Minor Victim 1 \u2013 \u201cVictor\u201d ...........................................................................4\nB. Minor Victim 2 \u2013 \u201cJake\u201d ..............................................................................6\nC. Minor Victim 3 \u2013 \u201cJohn\u201d..............................................................................8\nD. Minor Victim 4 \u2013 \u201cEric\u201d ............................................................................10\nE. Minor Victim 5 \u2013 \u201cJason\u201d ..........................................................................12\nF. Presentation of Child Pornography Evidence ............................................14\nIII. STATEMENT OF THE LAW ......................................................................15\nA. Other Legal Issues Involving Production of Child Pornography ..............16\nB. Other Legal Issues Involving Sex Trafficking a Minor .............................19\nC. Other Legal Issues Involving Enticement of a Minor................................22\nD. Expert Notice..............................................................................................24\nIV. OTHER EVIDENTIARY AND LEGAL ISSUES .......................................25\nA. Hearsay .......................................................................................................25\nB. Minor Victim Witnesses ............................................................................26\nC. Summary Witness and Witness in a Dual Role .........................................26\nD. Chain of Custody ........................................................................................28\nE. Forfeiture ....................................................................................................30\n\nii\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 3 of 34 PageID.1188\n\nBy and through the undersigned counsel, the government respectfully\n\nsubmits its Trial Brief to address matters that may arise in the course of trial.\n\nI. STATEMENT OF THE CASE\n\nA. Superseding Indictment\n\nOn July 25, 2024, a grand jury returned a twelve-count Superseding\n\nIndictment against Riley, charging him with production of child pornography, in\n\nviolation of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2251(a) (Counts 1 and 2 involving Minor Victim 1);\n\nproduction of child pornography, sex trafficking a minor, and distribution of child\n\npornography, in violation of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2251(a), \u00a7 1591(a)(1), and \u00a7 2252(a)(2)\n\n(Counts 3 through 5 involving Minor Victim 2); sex trafficking a minor,\n\nenticement of a minor, and receipt of child pornography, in violation of 18 U.S.C.\n\n\u00a7 1591(a)(1), \u00a7 2422(b), and \u00a7 2252(a)(2) (Counts 6 through 8 involving Minor\n\nVictim 3); sex trafficking and enticement of a minor, in violation of 18 U.S.C.\n\n\u00a7 1591(a)(1) and \u00a7 2422(b) (Counts 9 and 10 involving Minor Victim 4); sex\n\ntrafficking a minor, in violation of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1591(a)(1) (Count 11 involving\n\nMinor Victim 5); and possession of child pornography, in violation of 18 U.S.C.\n\n\u00a7 2252(a)(4)(B) (Count 12 involving child pornography images and videos on a\n\ncellphone, referred to as evidence item 1B10). ECF No. 70. Counts 1 through 7,\n\n10, and 11 also charge attempt allegations. Count 12 alleges that at least one of\n\n1\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 4 of 34 PageID.1189\n\nthe visual depictions Riley possessed was of a pre-pubescent minor or a minor\n\nunder twelve years old. Id. (18 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 2252(a)(4)(B), (b)(2)).\n\nB. Trial Status\n\nA jury trial is scheduled to begin on March 4, 2026. The parties have\n\nconferred with the Court and expect jury selection to take at least one full day.\n\nC. Pretrial Rulings\n\nDuring pretrial proceedings on January 22 and February 18, 2026, the Court\n\ndenied the following four motions to suppress in oral rulings: Motion to Suppress\n\nEvidence (1B15) No. 2 (ECF No. 135), Motion to Suppress Evidence No. 3\n\n(1B10) (ECF No. 136), Motion to Suppress Evidence No. 1 (1B1 and 1B2) (ECF\n\nNo. 134), and Motion to Suppress Evidence No. 4 (1B1) (ECF No. 162-1). At\n\ntrial, the government intends to present evidence from each of these electronic\n\ndevices.\n\nD. Pending Rulings\n\nMotions in limine are scheduled to be heard on February 25, 2026. The\n\ndefendant has filed one motion in limine to preclude narration during video\n\nevidence. ECF No. 166. The government has responded in ECF No. 169. The\n\ngovernment has also submitted a notice pursuant to Fed. R. Evid. 404(b). ECF\n\nNo. 165. The defendant filed a motion in limine to exclude some of that\n\n2\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 5 of 34 PageID.1190\n\nevidence, ECF No. 169, and the government will respond by February 21, 2026.\n\nThe government has also noticed its intent to introduce evidence pursuant to Fed.\n\nR. Evid. 414, ECF No. 165, to which the defendant has not objected.\n\nII. ANTICIPATED FACTS AND PRESENTATION AT TRIAL\n\nBeginning on a date unknown, but by at least the summer of 2019 through\n\nAugust 2020, the defendant, Darren Patrick Riley (\u201cRiley\u201d), engaged in a pattern\n\nof exploitative sexual conduct involving minor males. Riley would meet and\n\nbegin conversing with minor males, generally between 14 to 16 years old, on\n\nGrindr, which was an internet-based dating and communications application. At\n\nthat time, Riley was in his late thirties and living in Honolulu, Hawaii. Riley\n\nbegan communicating with the minors on Grindr in order to arrange sexual\n\nencounters with them. He would then often transition to communicating with the\n\nminors over text messages, or using other applications like Snapchat or Instagram.\n\nTo induce, persuade, and entice the minors, Riley would offer them things of\n\nvalue such as money, electronics, transportation, and drugs. Riley would then\n\nmeet the victims in person at hotel rooms, his Waikiki apartment, and in other\n\nlocations, such as his car. Although Riley engaged in these communications and\n\nencounters with more minors than are charged in the Superseding Indictment, the\n\ngovernment\u2019s evidence in its case-in-chief at trial will focus only on the five\n\n3\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 6 of 34 PageID.1191\n\ncharged minors\u2014Minor Victims 1 through 5, who will be addressed at trial by the\n\npseudonyms Victor, Jake, John, Eric, and Jason.\n\nA. Minor Victim 1 \u2013 \u201cVictor\u201d\n\nMinor Victim 1, Victor, is involved in Counts 1, 2, and 12. He was fourteen\n\nyears old and living in Oahu when he met Riley on Grindr in or around July 2019.\n\nRiley had at least two in-person sexual encounters with Minor Victim 1 on or\n\nabout July 5, 2019, and August 1, 2019. They engaged in communications\n\nrecovered on Riley\u2019s devices from July 2019 through February 2020. For\n\nexample, Riley tells Minor Victim 1: \u201cI\u2019ll give you at $300 if you come into town.\n\nDo it 2x and I\u2019ll buy you an XS Max 256gb. Or it\u2019s equivalent in cash.\u201d\n\nThe videos charged in Counts 1 and 2 took place during the July 5th\n\nencounter at the Embassy Suites hotel in Kapolei, Hawaii. Count 1 is a 56-second\n\nvideo in the hotel room that shows Riley engaged in a sex act with Minor Victim\n\n1. Count 2 is a 16-second video that shows Minor Victim 1 inserting an object\n\ninto his anus in a bathroom at the same hotel room. For the production counts,\n\nthe government must prove that Riley employed, used, persuaded, induced, or\n\n4\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 7 of 34 PageID.1192\n\nenticed Minor Victim 1 to engage in sexually explicit conduct for the purpose of\n\ncreating a visual depiction of such conduct. 1\n\nAt the beginning of the video in Count 1, Minor Victim 1 can be heard\n\nstating his age as fourteen in the recording. During this sexual encounter at the\n\nEmbassy Suites, Riley injected some unknown substance into Minor Victim 1\u2019s\n\nanus. Minor Victim 1 told Riley that he was a virgin before their sexual\n\nencounter. In messages, Minor Victim 1 told Riley that he had pain and discharge\n\nfrom his anus after the sexual encounter and was scared. Around the same time,\n\nin text messages between Riley and Shribx, Riley and Shribx discussed that Riley\n\nput drugs with lube, termed a \u201cbooty bump,\u201d into Minor Victim 1\u2019s anus without\n\nthe minor\u2019s knowledge. In February 2020, Riley and Shribx again discuss the\n\nchild pornography videos of Minor Victim 1 from the Embassy Suites, after\n\nShribx asked about \u201cthat one teen\u201d that Riley \u201cnootybumped [sic].\u201d\n\nThe evidence at trial will include: selections of the chats on Grindr and text\n\nmessages between Riley and Minor Victim 1 from Riley\u2019s devices; both charged\n\nand uncharged videos and images from Riley\u2019s devices of Minor Victim 1\n\nengaged in sexually explicit conduct at the hotel on July 5, 2019; Embassy Suites\n\n1\nThe statute also criminalizes coercing a minor to engage in sexually explicit\nconduct. See 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2251(a). However, the government does not intend to\nargue at trial that Riley coerced the victims for any of the production counts\u2014\nCounts 1 through 3.\n5\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 8 of 34 PageID.1193\n\nhotel records; pictures of the hotel room; text messages between Riley and\n\nanother individual identified as \u201cShribx\u201d where Riley discusses his sexual\n\nencounter with Minor Victim 1 and keeping the videos of the sexual encounter;\n\nthe birth certificate of Minor Victim 1; and testimony from a person who knows\n\nMinor Victim 1. The government does not anticipate that Minor Victim 1 will\n\ntestify at trial.\n\nB. Minor Victim 2 \u2013 \u201cJake\u201d\nCounts 3, 4, and 5 (production, sex trafficking, and distribution of child\n\npornography) involve Minor Victim 2, Jake. Riley met Minor Victim 2 on Grindr\n\nwhen Minor Victim 2 was 14 years old. Riley communicated with Minor Victim\n\n2 from at least August 2019 through in or around February 2020. Minor Victim 2\n\nwill testify, and the text communications will corroborate, that Riley knew Minor\n\nVictim 2 was a minor. For example, the day after their first sexual encounter, in\n\ntext messages, Riley and Minor Victim 2 discuss Minor Victim 2\u2019s freshman high\n\nschool orientation.\n\nRiley met with Minor Victim 2 in person for the first time at Riley\u2019s Waikiki\n\napartment on or about August 4, 2019 to engage in sexual contact. Riley\n\nprovided Minor Victim 2 with at least two pills. At the time, Minor Victim 2\n\nbelieved one of the pills to be the drug known as \u201cMolly\u201d (also known as ecstasy\n\nor MDMA) but did not know what the other pill was. Later, he found out from\n\n6\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 9 of 34 PageID.1194\n\nRiley that it was \u201cblue dominos\u201d or pressed meth, which is reflected in the text\n\nmessages exchanged between Riley and Minor Victim 2. Riley ultimately did\n\nengage in sexual acts with Minor Victim 2. Minor Victim 2 will describe how\n\nimpaired the drugs Riley gave him made him feel during the sex acts. During the\n\nencounter, Riley suggested that they record Riley and Minor Victim 2 engaged in\n\na sex act. There are two sexually explicit videos of this encounter\u2014the charged\n\nvideo in Count 3 and a second video that provides additional context and\n\ncorroborates Minor Victim 2\u2019s account\u2014as well as two pictures of the same.\n\nMinor Victim 2 will testify that Riley arranged transportation for Minor Victim 2\n\nusing a taxi or rideshare.\n\nAfter the encounter, in text messages recovered from Riley\u2019s devices, Riley\n\nasked Minor Victim 2 if Minor Victim 2 wanted \u201cpics or videos\u201d of their sexual\n\nencounter. Minor Victim 2 is expected to testify that Riley then sent Minor\n\nVictim 2 on Snapchat a copy of the video recorded at Riley\u2019s apartment on or\n\nabout August 4, 2019 of Riley and Minor Victim 2 engaged in a sex act. This is\n\nthe basis for Count 5 (distribution). Minor Victim 2 is expected to testify that\n\nRiley sent him the video a second time after more time had passed.\n\nFrom August 2019 to February 2020, Riley engaged in sexual contact with\n\nMinor Victim 2 multiple times at various locations, including Riley\u2019s apartment\n\nand in or around Minor Victim 2\u2019s home. Minor Victim 2 had to hide that he was\n7\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 10 of 34 PageID.1195\n\nmeeting up with Riley from his family. Minor Victim 2 will explain that prior to\n\nmeeting Riley the first time, he had only tried and used \u201cweed\u201d and not any other\n\nharder drugs. But that after Riley provided Minor Victim 2 with Molly and\n\nanother drug during their first sexual encounter in August 2019, Minor Victim 2\n\nbegan regularly using harder controlled substances, ultimately impacting his\n\nmental health. Riley offered and provided Minor Victim 2 with various types of\n\ndrugs on multiple occasions. Riley also gave Minor Victim 2 other items like\n\nurine and a kit to pass a drug test, as well as a cellphone. Riley regularly directed\n\nMinor Victim 2 to delete their conversation. Minor Victim 2 eventually blocked\n\nRiley from communicating with him.\n\nAt trial, the government will introduce selected text messages and Grindr\n\nchats found on Riley\u2019s electronic devices, the videos of Minor Victim 2 engaged\n\nin sexually explicit conduct described above and other images of Minor Victim 2\n\nengaged in sexually explicit conduct that were found on Riley\u2019s devices, other\n\nphotographs and records to corroborate the relevant events, and Minor Victim 2\u2019s\n\ntestimony.\n\nC. Minor Victim 3 \u2013 \u201cJohn\u201d\nCounts 6, 7, and 8 (sex trafficking, enticement, and receipt of child\n\npornography) involve Minor Victim 3, John. Minor Victim 2 was fourteen years\n\nold when he first met Riley online on Grindr in or around January 2020. Riley\n\n8\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 11 of 34 PageID.1196\n\ncommunicated with Minor Victim 3 through in or around August 2020. Shortly\n\nafter connecting on Grindr, in messages recovered from a device belonging to\n\nRiley, Minor Victim 3 disclosed to Riley that he was fifteen years old, which\n\nRiley knew and acknowledged and did not deter him from his attempts to\n\npersuade, induce, and entice Minor Victim 3 to meet up for sex. During their\n\ncommunications on Grindr and text message, Riley repeatedly offered Minor\n\nVictim 3 money, a cellphone, and other items to meet up. Riley also sent sexually\n\nexplicit images of himself and requested and received sexually explicit images\n\nfrom Minor Victim 3. Riley and Minor Victim 3 met in person for sexual\n\nencounters at least three times from January 2020 to August 2020. Minor Victim\n\n3 had to hide that he was meeting up with Riley from his family.\n\nThe first sexual encounter in January 2020 encounter was at Riley\u2019s Waikiki\n\napartment. Riley offered to arrange and pay for a rideshare to pick up Minor\n\nVictim 3 from his home and bring him to Riley\u2019s home. Minor Victim 4\n\nexpressed his anxiousness to Riley about using a rideshare and Riley repeatedly\n\nattempted to persuade and induce Minor Victim 4 to meet him. Ultimately, the\n\nUber Riley ordered would not take Minor Victim 4 because Minor Victim 4 was\n\nunderage. Riley then offered to get a Lyft or a taxi, even though Minor Victim 4\n\nexpressed further hesitation. Ultimately, Riley showed up near Minor Victim 4\u2019s\n\nhouse in a taxi to pick him up for the sexual encounter.\n\n9\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 12 of 34 PageID.1197\n\nDuring the January encounter at Riley\u2019s apartment, Minor Victim 3 will\n\ntestify that he and Riley had sexual contact. After that encounter, Minor Victim 3\n\ntold Riley that he was not interested in meeting up again, but Riley attempted to\n\npersuade Minor Victim 3 by offering him $300 for another sexual encounter.\n\nRiley and Minor Victim 3 reconnected in May 2020 and met up again for\n\ntheir second sexual encounter after Minor Victim 3 snuck out from his home to\n\nmeet Riley. Finally, in August 2020, Riley engaged in another sexual encounter\n\nwith Minor Victim 3. Just prior to meeting up, on or about May 16, 2020, during\n\ntheir text communications, Riley sent Minor Victim 3 additional sexually explicit\n\nimages of himself and Minor Victim 3 responded by sending a sexually explicit\n\nimage of himself. That image of Minor Victim 3 is the basis for the receipt\n\ncharge in Count 8.\n\nAt trial, the government will introduce selected Grindr messages and text\n\nmessages between Riley and Minor Victim 3 found on Riley\u2019s electronic devices,\n\nthe sexually explicit image of Minor Victim 3 sent to Riley in a text message\n\nfound on one of Riley\u2019s devices, other records to corroborate the relevant events\n\nsuch as records from rideshare apps, and Minor Victim 3\u2019s testimony.\n\nD. Minor Victim 4 \u2013 \u201cEric\u201d\nCounts 9 and 10 (sex trafficking and enticement of a minor) involved Minor\n\nVictim 4, Eric. Minor Victim 4 was fifteen years old when Riley met him online\n10\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 13 of 34 PageID.1198\n\non Grindr in or around August 2019. On August 1, 2019, on Grindr, Minor\n\nVictim 4 asked \u201cdo u care if im young.\u201d Riley responded: \u201cYes I do. I\u2019ll think it\u2019s\n\neven hotter.\u201d Minor Victim 4 then told Riley he was 15 years old and Riley\n\nresponded: \u201cAnd I want to impress you more\u201d; \u201cDamn that\u2019s really hot.\u201d Riley\n\ncommunicated with Minor Victim 4 through in or around August 2020.\n\nRepeatedly, to meet up for sexual encounters, Riley offered Minor Victim 4\n\nmoney, an iPhone, and other items. Riley knew that Minor Victim 4\u2019s family\n\nstruggled financially and took advantage of Minor Victim 4\u2019s financial need. For\n\nexample, he guilted Minor Victim 4 about the money and the phone he provided\n\nMinor Victim 4 when Minor Victim 4 could not meet up with Riley.\n\nBetween August 2019 and August 2020, Riley and Minor Victim 4 met\n\nmultiple times at various locations for sexual encounters, such as the beach,\n\nRiley\u2019s car, and a hotel that Riley paid for.2 Riley also offered and arranged for\n\ntransportation, such as Uber, for Minor Victim 4 to get to and from the various\n\nlocations where they had sexual encounters. Initially, Minor Victim 4 told Riley\n\nthat he was \u201cto[o] young\u201d because \u201c[y]ou have to be 18 to get into an uber.\u201d In\n\ntheir messages, Riley was persistent in repeatedly attempting to induce, persuade,\n\nand entice Minor Victim 4 to meet up with him for sexual encounters. During\n\n2\nRiley had previously used the same hotel booking for an encounter with Minor\nVictim 1.\n11\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 14 of 34 PageID.1199\n\ntheir encounters, Minor Victim 4 had to hide that he was meeting up with Riley\n\nfrom his family.\n\nAt trial, the government will introduce selected text messages and Grindr\n\nchats found on Riley\u2019s electronic devices, other records to corroborate the\n\nrelevant events, and Minor Victim 4\u2019s testimony.\n\nE. Minor Victim 5 \u2013 \u201cJason\u201d\n\nCount 11 (sex trafficking) relates to Minor Victim 5, Jason, who was 16\n\nyears old when Riley met him on Grindr in or around July 2020. Between July\n\nand August 2020, Riley offered Minor Victim 5 money, clothes, and a phone for\n\nsex acts. For example, in a text message on July 9, 2020, Riley told Minor Victim\n\n5: \u201cI could pick u up get us a hotezl [sic] in your area. I could manage whatever\n\nyou want. Even a new iPhone if you wanted.\u201d Riley then stated: \u201cWe can go\n\ntogether to apple and buy it\u201d; \u201cThen we could meet up my place or better would\n\nbe hotel room that I\u2019ll get us.\u201d When Minor Victim 5 expressed some concern\n\nthat Riley might murder him, Riley responded: \u201cOmfg why would I murder\n\nsomeone I want to be with\u201d; \u201cI\u2019m not even into anything pain or rape related! It\u2019s\n\nbetter to be wanted by a guy.\u201d\n\nRiley met Minor Victim 5 multiple times for sexual encounters at various\n\nlocations, including a community center near Minor Victim 5\u2019s house, a hotel,\n\n12\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 15 of 34 PageID.1200\n\nand parking lot. In messages, on August 14, 2020, after a sexual encounter,\n\nMinor Victim 5 expressed concern to Riley that he had contracted a sexually\n\ntransmitted infection from him and told Riley he was \u201cscared.\u201d Riley then offered\n\nto get Minor Victim 5 prescription from a pharmacist friend and said he knew\n\nabout a clinic \u201cthat will keep it confidential for minors.\u201d Riley also gave Minor\n\nVictim 5 $500 during one of their encounters in a hotel in Waikiki. During that\n\nencounter, Riley was persistent in getting Minor Victim 5 to engage in a sex act\n\nwith him. Riley and Minor Victim 5 ultimately ceased communications after a\n\nsecurity guard encountered Riley and Minor Victim 5 in a restricted stairwell area\n\nof a building in Waikiki and contacted police. Minor Victim 5 is expected to\n\ntestify that Riley was trying to get Minor Victim 5 to go with Riley in Riley\u2019s car\n\nwhen security approached them, but Minor Victim 5 did not and waited for the\n\npolice. During their encounters, Minor Victim 5 had to hide that he was meeting\n\nup with Riley from his family. And Riley directed Minor Victim 5 to \u201c[b]e sure\n\nto erase our messages okay.\u201d\n\nAt trial, the government will introduce selected text messages found on\n\nRiley\u2019s electronic devices, other records to corroborate the relevant events, and\n\nMinor Victim 5\u2019s testimony.\n\n13\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 16 of 34 PageID.1201\n\nF. Presentation of Child Pornography Evidence\nMultiple of Riley\u2019s offenses involve child pornography\u2014Counts 1 through 3\n\n(production), Count 4 (distribution), Count 8 (receipt), and Count 12 (possession).\n\nThe parties have previously submitted a stipulation regarding the logistics of\n\npresenting child pornography (also known as \u201cchild sexual abuse material\u201d or\n\n\u201cCSAM\u201d) evidence to the jury. ECF No. 142. The government intends to use a\n\ncombination of playing very short CSAM videos, playing clips of longer CSAM\n\nvideos, showing storyboards of CSAM videos,3 and redacted screenshots to\n\nstreamline the efficient presentation of this evidence.\n\nThe three charged videos in Counts 1 through 3 were located on multiple\n\ndevices that will be used at trial (1B1, 1B2, 1B10, and 1B15), as well as in\n\nRiley\u2019s iCloud account. However, the government only intends to admit and\n\npublish to the jury the videos from one device per count. The government\u2019s\n\nforensic and/or summary witnesses will explain that the same videos were also\n\nlocated on other electronic devices. The distribution and receipt charges each\n\ninvolve the presentation of one image of child pornography at trial\u2014 a screenshot\n\nof the video in Count 3 (Count 4) and an image Riley received from Minor Victim\n\n3 (Count 8).\n\n3\nA storyboard is a compilation of representative screenshots from a video.\n14\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 17 of 34 PageID.1202\n\nThe possession of child pornography count (Count 12) relates to the cell\n\nphone seized from Riley when he was arrested for absconding from his federal\n\nsupervision on December 14, 2022 (evidence item 1B10). The government\n\nintends to admit limited evidence of the voluminous amount of child pornography\n\nvideos and images contained on 1B10 to prove Count 12 (possession of child\n\npornography, and of a minor under twelve years old or of a prepubescent minor).\n\nThe government will admit the three videos charged in Counts 1 through 3, as\n\nwell as other images/videos related to those counts (images/videos of the same\n\nvictims, taken during the same encounter, which are also relevant to prove\n\nelements of the production offenses). In order to establish that the defendant\n\npossessed material that included prepubescent minors or minors under the age of\n\n12, the government will admit no more than four videos of unknown prepubescent\n\nminors and/or minors under twelve years old. To prove knowledge and provide\n\nthe jury with a full picture of the offense, the government also intends to\n\nintroduce and admit a list of video and image file paths that were located on the\n\n1B10 cellphone and identified to be child pornography.\n\nIII. STATEMENT OF THE LAW\n\nThe parties filed joint proposed jury instructions on February 11, 2026. ECF\n\nNo. 173. The government submits that these instructions correctly state the law\n\nfor the charged offenses.\n\n15\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 18 of 34 PageID.1203\n\nA. Other Legal Issues Involving Production of Child Pornography\n\nThe sexual exploitation of children statute, commonly known as production\n\nof child pornography, criminalizes:\n\nAny person who employs, uses, persuades, induces, entices, or coerces any\nminor to engage in . . . with the intent that such minor engage in, any sexually\nexplicit conduct for the purpose of producing any visual depiction of such\nconduct . . . .\n18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2251(a).\nThe Ninth Circuit has \u201cadopted a broad definition\u201d of what it means to \u201cuse\u201d\n\na minor for the purposes of the statute. United States v. Hutton, 159 F.4th 636\n\n(9th Cir. 2025). The court has looked to the common dictionary definition of\n\n\u201cuses\u201d and no more is required than \u201cactive conduct\u201d that results in the production\n\nof child pornography. Id. Thus, the positioning of camera to capture sexually\n\nexplicit conduct, for example, would meet this definition. See id.\n\nThe production of the visual depiction does not need to be the defendant's\n\nsole purpose under \u00a7 2251(a), but rather a \u201cdominant, significant, or motivating\n\npurpose.\u201d See United States v. Rosenow, 50 F.4th 715, 739\u201340 (2022). Just\n\nbecause Riley may also have engaged in the sexual conduct with Minor Victims 1\n\nand 2 for some other purpose, that other purpose does not negate that he also\n\n\u201cemploye[d], use[d], persuade[d], induce[d], entice[d], or coerce[d] any minor to\n\nengage in . . . any sexually explicit conduct . . . for the purpose of producing any\n\n16\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 19 of 34 PageID.1204\n\nvisual depiction of such conduct.\u201d 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2251(a)(1). See Rosenow, 50 F.4th\n\nat 739\u201340 (affirming district court where \u201cRosenow requested an instruction\n\nstating that the \u2018purpose\u2019 element was satisfied only if the government proved that\n\nhe \u2018would not have acted but for his desire to produce a visual depiction of the\n\nsexually-explicit conduct.\u2019 The district court rejected Rosenow\u2019s proposed\n\ninstruction and instead instructed the jury that the government must prove that\n\n\u2018producing a visual depiction of a minor engaged in sexually explicit conduct\u2019\n\nwas Rosenow\u2019s \u2018dominant, significant or motivating\u2019 purpose, not that it was his\n\n\u2018sole purpose.\u2019\u201d).\n\nThe defendant also is not required to be the one who actually took the video\n\nto meet the elements of the offense. Rather, the statute only requires that the\n\ndefendant employed, used persuaded, induced, enticed, or coerced a minor to\n\nengage in sexually explicit conduct for the purpose of producing any visual\n\ndepiction of that conduct. See 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2251(a)(1). See, e.g., United States v.\n\nFields, 2020 WL 3799227, at *4 (E.D. Ky. July 7, 2020), aff\u2019d, 2022 WL 633847\n\n(6th Cir. Mar. 4, 2022) (\u201c[T]he Court\u2019s definition of \u201cuses\u201d is consistent with\n\nparagraph (3) of Sixth Circuit Pattern Instruction 16.01, which provides: \u2018It is not\n\nnecessary that the government prove that the defendant took the pictures.\u2019\u201d (citing\n\nUnited States v. Daniels, 653 F.3d 399, 408 (6th Cir. 2001) (Defendant violated\n\n\u00a7 2251(a) even if he did not actually take the picture because he persuaded,\n\n17\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 20 of 34 PageID.1205\n\ninduced, or coerced the victim to engage in sexually explicit conduct for the\n\npurpose of producing a visual depiction.))); United States v. Blank, 2015 WL\n\n4041408 (D. Md. June 30, 2015), aff\u2019d, 659 F. App\u2019x 727 (4th Cir. 2016) (finding\n\nthat even if the victim hatched the idea to create the images, it is not a defense to a\n\n\u00a7 2251(a) production charge because the government could prove that the\n\ndefendant \u201cused\u201d the victim for the purpose of producing child pornography).\n\nThe parties agree that a minor\u2019s willingness or consent is not a defense to the\n\ncharges, including the production charge. Thus, even if a minor testified that he\n\nvoluntarily engaged in recording a sexually explicit video, it would not negate the\n\ndefendant\u2019s guilt because the statutory focus is on the defendant\u2019s actions. See,\n\ne.g., United States v. Mayer, 674 F.3d 942 (8th Cir. 2012) (production case where\n\ndefendant claimed victim initiated sexual discussions, sent first sexually explicit\n\nphoto, and victim testified she took and sent explicit photos of herself \u201cof her own\n\nfree will\u201d did not negate finding that the evidence proved the defendant enticed or\n\ninduced the minor to engage in sexually explicit conduct for the purpose of\n\nproducing a visual depiction where he sent numerous sexually explicit messages\n\nand sexual photos of himself, and requested sexual photos of the victim).\n\nFinally, the production of child pornography statute as well as the other child\n\nexploitation statutes do not require the government to prove a defendant\u2019s\n\nknowledge of the interstate or foreign commerce element. See United States v.\n\n18\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 21 of 34 PageID.1206\n\nSheldon, 755 F.3d 1047, 1048 (9th Cir. 2014). Rather, the government is only\n\nrequired to prove that the child pornography was produced with materials that had\n\ntraveled in interstate commerce or were transmitted in or affecting interstate\n\ncommerce. Both bases will be part of the trial evidence to prove the production\n\ncounts in this case. Further, to meet this element, the \u201cmaterial\u201d for production\n\ndoes not need to be limited \u201cto the moment of recording, and that \u2018production\u2019\n\ncan encompass embodying the images or video in digital media, such as a hard\n\ndrive or disk.\u201d United States v. Pattee, 820 F.3d 496, 511 (2d Cir. 2016) (citing\n\nagreement with cases from First and Seventh Circuits).\n\nB. Other Legal Issues Involving Sex Trafficking a Minor\n\nThe sex trafficking counts of Counts 4, 6, 9, and 11 relate to Minor Victims\n\n2, 3, 4, and 5, respectively. Although the government anticipates that the\n\nevidence at trial will show that Riley did engage in sex acts with the minors, that\n\nproof is not necessary to prove the completed offense. Moreover, the government\n\nhas also charged an attempt for each of these counts. To prove the offense of sex\n\ntrafficking a minor, in violation of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1591(a)(1), an actual sex act is not\n\nrequired to have occurred. United States v. Brooks, 610 F.3d 1186, 1196\u201397 (9th\n\nCir. 2010). Rather, the exchange of something of value can be in anticipation of a\n\nsex act, for example. United States v. Hornbuckle, 784 F.3d 549, 553 (9th Cir.\n\n2015) (The offense \u201cdoes not require commission of a sex act because it can be\n19\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 22 of 34 PageID.1207\n\nbased on an exchange of money in anticipation of the sex act.\u201d); United States v.\n\nChang Ru Meng Backman, 817 F.3d 662, 666 (9th Cir. 2016) (\u201cCausation is not\n\nan element in a \u00a7 1591(a) prosecution, because a commercial sex act need not\n\neven occur: \u2018Case law makes clear that commission of a sex act or sexual contact\n\nis not an element of a conviction under 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1591.\u2019 \u2018What the statute\n\nrequires is that the defendant know in the sense of being aware of an established\n\nmodus operandi that will in the future coerce a prostitute to engage in\n\nprostitution.\u2019 Because the statute does not require commission of a sex act, the\n\ncourt correctly refused to require the jury to find that Defendant caused a sex act\n\nto occur.\u201d (internal citations omitted)).\n\nThe offense also requires that the defendant knew or recklessly disregarded\n\nthat the victim had not attained the age of 18 years and would be caused to engage\n\nin a commercial sex act. The government does not need to prove that the\n\ndefendant actually knew or recklessly disregarded the victim\u2019s age, where the\n\ndefendant had a reasonable opportunity to observe the victim. 18 U.S.C. \u00a7\n\n1591(c). The statute broadly defines \u201ccommercial sex act\u201d as \u201cany sex act, on\n\naccount of which anything of value is given to or received by any person.\u201d 18\n\nU.S.C. \u00a7 1591. The phrase \u201canything of value\u201d is not limited to a pure exchange\n\nof money. Courts have construed this phrase broadly, holding that a wide range\n\nof tangible and intangible things may constitute \u201canything of value.\u201d See, e.g.,\n\n20\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 23 of 34 PageID.1208\n\nUnited States v. Wills, 40 F.4th 330, 333 (5th Cir.) (generally affirming \u00a7 1591\n\nconviction where defendant gave victim\u2019s mother money and gifts), cert. denied,\n\n143 S. Ct. 464 (2022); United States v. Raniere, 55 F.4th 354, 360-62, 364-65 (2d\n\nCir. 2022) (holding that \u201c\u2018anything of value\u2019 need not have a monetary or\n\nfinancial component\u201d); United States v. Adams, 789 F.3d 903, 906-07 (8th Cir.\n\n2015) (affirming \u00a7 1591 conviction where mother received morphine pills for sex\n\nact performed on minor daughter); see also United States v. Marmolejo, 89 F.3d\n\n1185, 1192-93 (5th Cir. 1996) (\u201canything of value\u201d in 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 666(a)(1)(B)\n\nincludes intangible things). The trial evidence will adduce a variety of things to\n\nmeet this element of the offense, such as money, cellphones, drugs and pills, clean\n\n(i.e., drug-free) urine, meals, a place to stay, and free transportation.\n\nThe government also need only prove that Riley\u2019s trafficking of the minors\n\nhad \u201ca de minimis effect on interstate commerce.\u201d United States v. Walls, 784\n\nF.3d 543, 548 (9th Cir. 2015). These actions can all be considered in the\n\naggregate. Courts have repeatedly held that the use of phones, hotels, and\n\nrideshares or taxis are means and facilities of interstate commerce sufficient to\n\nprovide jurisdiction. See, e.g., United States v. Phea, 755 F.3d 255, 266 (5th Cir.\n\n2014) (\u201ctelephones, the Internet, and hotels that service interstate travelers are all\n\nmeans or facilities of interstate commerce sufficient to establish the requisite\n\ninterstate nexus\u201d); United States v. Young, 955 F.3d 608, 612-13 (7th Cir. 2020)\n\n21\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 24 of 34 PageID.1209\n\n(affirming jury instruction stating the interstate commerce element would be met\n\nif defendant \u201cused hotels that serve interstate travelers\u201d or \u201cused the Internet to\n\nplace advertisements\u201d); United States v. Baston, 818 F.3d 651, 664 (11th Cir.\n\n2016) (holding defendant's \u201cuse of phones, the Internet, hotels, and buses\n\nfacilitated his trafficking of [the victim], so his conduct was \u2018in commerce,\u201d\u2019 even\n\nif the victim was trafficked exclusively intrastate); cf. Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc.\n\nv. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 248 (1964) (holding that the local operations of a\n\nmotel affect interstate commerce).\n\nC. Other Legal Issues Involving Enticement of a Minor\n\nCounts 7 and 10 charge the enticement of Minor Victims 3 and 4\n\nrespectively. As detailed in full in the joint proposed jury instructions, this offense\n\nrequires the government to show that the defendant used any facility or means of\n\ninterstate or foreign commerce (here the internet and cell phones) to knowingly\n\npersuade, induce, or entice the minor victims to engage in sexual activity for which\n\nany person can be charged with an offense. See 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2422(b). Although\n\ncovered by the statute, the government does not intend to argue that the defendant\n\ncoerced the minor victims. As stipulated by the parties, the consent or voluntary\n\nparticipation of the minor victims is not a defense to this charge. See ECF No.\n\n164. Whether a minor is \u201cwilling\u201d to engage in the sexual activity is irrelevant.\n\nSee United States v. Eller, 57 F.4th 1117, 1121 (9th Cir. 2023) (\u201cWhether Eller\u2019s\n22\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 25 of 34 PageID.1210\n\nintended victims were \u2018willing\u2019 to engage in these acts is ultimately\n\nirrelevant . . . .\u201d); United States v. Dhingra, 371 F.3d 557, 567 (9th Cir. 2004)\n\n(\u201cThe victim\u2019s willingness to engage in sexual activity is irrelevant \u2026 .\u201d); United\n\nStates v. Waqar, 997 F.3d 481, 487\u2013488 (2d Cir. 2021) ( \u201c[W]e hold that 18 U.S.C.\n\n\u00a7 2422(b) imposes no requirement that an individual endeavor to \u2018transform or\n\novercome\u2019 the will of his intended victim.\u201d); United States v. Chip, 762 F. App\u2019x\n\n86, 88\u201389 (3d Cir. 2019) (rejecting defendant\u2019s argument that he could not have\n\nviolated Section 2422(b) because the victim \u201cdemonstrated an \u2018unwavering\u2019\n\ninterest in a sexual encounter\u201d and explaining that \u201c\u00a7 2422(b) focuses on [the\n\ndefendant\u2019s] conduct\u201d). Rather the focus must always remain on the defendant\u2019s\n\nconduct. United States v. Macapagal, 56 F.4th 742, 747 (9th Cir. 2022) (affirming\n\ndistrict court\u2019s instruction that a \u201cminor\u2019s willingness to engage in sexual activity\n\nis irrelevant to the elements of\u201d Section 2422(b) and explaining that \u201cthe relevant\n\ninquiry is the conduct of the defendant, not the minor.\u201d (internal punctuation\n\nomitted)); Dhingra, 371 F.3d at 561 (\u201cThe focus of [Section 2422(b)] is on the\n\nactor and the intent of his actions, and thus liability depends on the audience for\n\nwhom the communication is intended and the conduct the communication seeks to\n\nprovoke.\u201d).\n\n23\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 26 of 34 PageID.1211\n\nD. Expert Notice\nThe government has noticed three experts as potential witnesses in its case-\n\nin-chief pursuant to the disclosures required in Fed. R. Crim. P. 16. Two experts\n\nare FBI computer forensic specialists who work with CART. Special Agent\n\nWynton El will testify about the devices that were seized in Honolulu, Hawaii,\n\n1B10 and 1B15. Former Special Agent Perry Woo retired from FBI in December\n\n2025. He will testify about the devices that were seized in Los Angeles,\n\nCalifornia, 1B1 and 1B2.\n\nIn addition to the expert disclosures, the government intends to submit\n\ncertifications pursuant to Fed. R. Evid. 902(14) to expedite the trial testimony. It\n\nis also the government\u2019s understanding that the extraction of the four devices will\n\nnot be a contested issue at trial. Therefore, the government anticipates that the\n\nexperts\u2019 testimony on the process of extraction will be limited. Rather, the\n\nexperts will focus their testimony on attribution of the devices and the\n\nidentification, location, and data regarding the relevant items contained within.\n\nThe third expert is FBI Special Agent Dan O\u2019Donnell. SA O\u2019Donnell works\n\nin the FBI\u2019s Behavior Analysis Unit. If called to testify, he would be testifying as\n\na blind expert, that is, he is not familiar with the specific facts of this case. His\n\ntestimony would discuss the \u201cgrooming\u201d process with a focus on online grooming\n\nand the impacts of grooming on victim behavior. This includes how an offender\n\n24\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 27 of 34 PageID.1212\n\nidentifies a victim, techniques offenders use to exploit victims and reduce the\n\nlikelihood victims will disclose the abuse, and the impact that process has on\n\nvictim behavior.\n\nIV. OTHER EVIDENTIARY AND LEGAL ISSUES\n\nA. Hearsay\n\nThe government anticipates introducing Riley\u2019s own out-of-court statements\n\ncaptured in messages, on video, and relayed directly to minor victims. Riley\u2019s\n\nstatements are not hearsay. They are admissible under Federal Rule of Evidence\n\n801(d)(2)(A) as admissions of a party opponent. See United States v. Warren, 25\n\nF.3d 890, 895 (9th Cir. 1994). Many of Riley\u2019s statements are captured in chat and\n\ntext messages with others\u2014specifically the minor victims and the person identified\n\nas \u201cShribx.\u201d The government expects to introduce the victim\u2019s and Shribx\u2019s\n\nstatements in the messages, as well as what the victims say on the video. This\n\nevidence is admissible \u201cto place [Riley\u2019s] responses into context\u201d and to make\n\nRiley\u2019s statements \u201c\u2018intelligible to the jury and recognizable as admissions.\u2019\u201d\n\nUnited States v. Wills, 346 F.3d 476, 490 (4th Cir. 2003) (quoting United States v.\n\nLemonakis, 485, F.2d 941, 948 (D.C. Cir. 1973)). Further, those statements are\n\nrelevant to prove Riley\u2019s knowledge of certain elements of the offenses, such as his\n\n25\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 28 of 34 PageID.1213\n\nbelief regarding the age of the victims with respect to possession, distribution, and\n\nreceipt offenses.\n\nB. Minor Victim Witnesses\n\nThe government anticipates the testimony of minor victims who are\n\nidentified in the Superseding Indictment. The parties have submitted stipulations\n\nregarding the testimony of these victims. First, the parties have stipulated as to the\n\nuse of pseudonyms to refer to Minor Victims 1 through 5 at trial. See ECF No.\n\n164. The parties have also stipulated that the minor victims will not be shown any\n\nunredacted child pornography during their testimony. Id.\n\nC. Summary Witness and Witness in a Dual Role\n\nThe government anticipates that FBI Special Agent (\u201cSA\u201d) Gabriel Keown\n\nwill testify in the mode of both a fact witness and a summary witness. SA Keown\n\nwill testify about the seizure of 1B10 and 1B15 as a percipient witness. On\n\nOctober 20, 2022, in Honolulu, Hawaii, SA Keown took custody of 1B15 from\n\nEzekial Aquino. On December 14, 2022, in Honolulu, Hawaii, SA Keown was\n\npart of Riley\u2019s arrest at the Marco Polo residential apartment building. During the\n\narrest, SA Keown recovered 1B10. Both 1B10 and 1B15 are in FBI custody and\n\nSA Keown will serve as a chain of custody witness for these devices. Additionally,\n\n26\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 29 of 34 PageID.1214\n\nSA Keown is expected to testify regarding his personal observations of hotel\n\nrooms rented by Riley during the commission of the charged offenses.\n\nSA Keown is also expected to testify in the mode of a summary witness to\n\nintroduce certain business records (e.g., hotel and ride share records that will not\n\nbe admitted through a separate witness), public records (e.g., minor victim\u2019s\n\nidentifying documents), Riley\u2019s messages with minors and Shribx, and the videos\n\nand images of child pornography that will be submitted to the jury. The purpose of\n\nSA Keown\u2019s testimony is not to summarize the testimony of the minors. In fact,\n\nhe likely will testify before the minor victims.\n\nThe chat communications between Riley and the minors involve thousands\n\nof messages and thousands of pages of texts. The government will be admitting\n\nonly a select sample of those messages to provide context, a coherent narrative,\n\ncorroborate the minors\u2019 testimony, and prove the elements of the charges in the\n\nSuperseding Indictment. The government may offer the entirety of some message\n\nthreads (meaning the entire recovered conversation from first message to last), but\n\nfor others the government may offer only selected, relevant excerpts. The\n\ngovernment does not intend to read into evidence entire exchanges. Rather, the\n\ngovernment anticipates it will review with SA Keown key exchanges from\n\nmessages. The government then anticipates presenting only limited messages\n\nthrough the minor victims where necessary to provide context, clarity, or\n\n27\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 30 of 34 PageID.1215\n\nexplanation about the purpose or substance of the specific messages between Riley\n\nand the minors.\n\nThe government is consulting with the defense regarding witness order and\n\nadmission of evidence. The government has discussed with defense counsel that it\n\nmay need to call SA Keown two or three times in its case-in-chief to testify about\n\nseparate topics. First, to testify as to chain of custody in the beginning of the case.\n\nThen again, after the forensic experts have testified, SA Keown would testify as to\n\nsummary topics discussed above. The forensic experts will set the foundation to\n\nadmit the evidence from Riley\u2019s electronic devices. SA Keown may also be called\n\nagain to present the child pornography evidence involving the charged minors.\n\nThe government and defense counsel continue to work to ensure the orderly and\n\nefficient presentation of evidence.\n\nD. Chain of Custody\n\nThe government will introduce evidence from forensic extractions of four\n\nelectronic devices that are in the government\u2019s custody\u20141B1 (iPhone 11 Pro\n\nMax), 1B2 (iPad), 1B10 (iPhone 13 Pro Max), 1B15 (iPhone XR)\u2014and the\n\nphysical electronic devices. 1B1 and 1B2 were seized by DEA agents in\n\nconnection with Riley\u2019s drug trafficking arrest at the Los Angeles airport (\u201cLAX\u201d)\n\n28\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 31 of 34 PageID.1216\n\non September 11, 2020.4 For these devices, at least five chain of custody witnesses\n\nmay testify. DEA Agent Sean Springer would testify about the seizure of the green\n\nsuitcase at the Honolulu, Hawaii, airport that contained the 1B2. DEA Agent\n\nShawna Candelaria would testify about the seizure of the cellphone 1B1 during\n\nRiley\u2019s arrest at LAX and taking custody of the green suitcase and 1B2 in Los\n\nAngeles. FBI Agent Natalie Bruford would testify about the transfer of custody of\n\n1B1 and 1B2 from DEA to FBI in Los Angeles, California, for the purposes of the\n\nchild exploitation investigation that led to the charges in this case. Former FBI\n\nAgent Perry Woo would testify about taking custody of the devices for the forensic\n\nextractions. And finally, FBI Agent Gabe Keown would testify about the transfer\n\nof custody of the devices from FBI in Los Angeles to FBI in Honolulu.\n\n1B15 and 1B10 were seized in Honolulu, Hawaii. 1B15 was seized on\n\nOctober 20, 2022, in Honolulu, Hawaii, after Riley had absconded from supervised\n\nrelease. 1B10 was seized on December 14, 2022, in Honolulu, Hawaii, during\n\nRiley\u2019s arrest on federal supervised release and state violations. FBI Agent Gabe\n\nKeown will testify regarding the seizure and custody of these devices.\n\nIt is well understood that the government need not establish all links in the\n\nchain of custody of an item or call every person who was in a position to come into\n\n4\nIn light of the recent evidentiary hearing held regarding the seizure of these\ndevices, the government does not restate the facts here.\n29\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 32 of 34 PageID.1217\n\ncontact with it. See Gallego v. United States, 276 F.2d 914, 917 (9th Cir. 1960).\n\nAny alleged gaps in the chain of custody go to the weight of the evidence rather\n\nthan to its admissibility. United States v. Matta-Ballasteros, 71 F.3d 754 (9th Cir.\n\n1995). The government is continuing to confer with defense counsel regarding\n\nthese matters, and the parties may be able to reach an agreement that obviates the\n\nneed for the government to call certain witnesses.\n\nE. Forfeiture\nThe Superseding Indictment includes three forfeiture allegations to cover the\n\ncounts charged. ECF No. 70. The government is not requesting the jury be\n\nretained to determine forfeiture. See Fed. R. Crim. P. 32.2(b)(5). In the absence\n\nof any such request, the Court can determine forfeiture. Fed. R. Crim. P.\n\n32.2(b)(1). The government expects the trial evidence alone will be sufficient to\n\nsupport a forfeiture finding here. See Fed. R. Crim. P. 32.2(b)(1)(B) (allowing the\n\ncourt to rely on evidence already in the record). See also Libretti v. United States,\n\n516 U.S. 29, 49 (1995).\n\n\\\\\n\n\\\\\n\n\\\\\n\n30\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 33 of 34 PageID.1218\n\nDATED: February 20, 2026, at Honolulu, Hawaii.\n\nRespectfully Submitted,\n\nKENNETH M. SORENSON\nUnited States Attorney\nDistrict of Hawaii\n\nBy /s/ Rebecca A. Perlmutter\nREBECCA A. PERLMUTTER\nAssistant U.S. Attorney\nGWENDELYNN BILLS\nTrial Attorney\n\n31\nCase 1:23-cr-00071-JMS Document 188 Filed 02/20/26 Page 34 of 34 PageID.1219\n\nCERTIFICATE OF SERVICE\n\nI hereby certify that, on the dates and by the methods of service noted\n\nbelow, a true and correct copy of the foregoing was served on the following:\n\nServed via CM/ECF Notification of Counsel of Record:\nAndrew Kennedy, Esq.\nAttorney for Defendant\n\nDATED: February 20, 2026, at Honolulu, Hawaii.\n\n/s/ Rebecca A. Perlmutter\nRebecca A. Perlmutter\nUnited States Attorney\u2019s Office\nDistrict of Hawaii\n\n32"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
